# ── Line endings ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Important: without this line, `git status` once showed 165 files as
#    "modified" although not a single character had changed (50,297 lines
#    added, 50,297 removed, all of it CR). The repo is touched from both
#    Windows (the owner's PC) and macOS, and when an editor on one side writes
#    CRLF, the other side sees the whole repo as changed.
#
# The real danger is not the annoyance but the camouflage: a genuine change
#    hidden inside that fake diff would go unnoticed in review. Git always
#    stores LF and checkouts also produce LF, so both sides have the same bytes.
#
# Careful: `.ps1` files stay fine with this - PowerShell accepts LF, and what it
#    really needs is the **BOM**, not the line ending (deploy/README.md › "The
#    scripts in this folder"). The BOM is a byte inside the file, so this rule
#    does not touch it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Careful: `.sh` files run under bash on the VPS. If CRLF sneaks in, `\r` becomes
#    part of the command and fails with `$'\r': command not found`, an error
#    that gives no hint of the cause.
*.sh text eol=lf
